From: ms178 irqbalance tuning
Subject: [PATCH v2] irqbalance: fix UB and uninitialised reads, remove two
 O(n^2) hot paths, hardware popcount, working adaptive interval

Supersedes ms178-1.patch v1. Every change below is backed by a measurement or
a proof; see AUDIT-v2.md for the commands and the raw numbers.

============================ CORRECTNESS ============================

1. sock_handle(): recvmsg() does not NUL-terminate, but every command is
   parsed with g_str_has_prefix()/strlen(), i.e. as a C string. The 16 KiB
   buffer is uninitialised stack, so the parser ran past the received data.
   Demonstrated: a 5-byte "stats" message yields strlen(buff) == 70.
   Fixed by terminating at recv_size (and rejecting recv_size >= sizeof).

2. sock_handle(): the three "settings ..." handlers compute
       malloc(recv_size - strlen(prefix) + 1)
       strncpy(dst, src, recv_size - strlen(prefix))
   with no check that recv_size >= strlen(prefix). Because the prefix test
   reads the buffer as a C string it can succeed while recv_size is shorter,
   and the subtraction then wraps: for recv_size 13 vs prefix 14 the copy
   length becomes 18446744073709551615. Guarded explicitly.
   NOTE: I could not trigger this empirically (the stack usually happens to
   contain a NUL), so it is fixed as defence in depth rather than a
   demonstrated live exploit. Bug 1 above IS demonstrated.

3. bitmap.h fls(): shifted a signed int into the sign bit -- undefined
   behaviour. UBSan on the running daemon reports
     "left shift of 805306368 by 2 places cannot be represented in 'int'".
   Reproduced on unpatched upstream, absent after the fix. Reimplemented on
   __builtin_clz: proven identical over 1,376,188 inputs, and it lowers to a
   single BSR instead of five dependent compare-and-shift steps.

4. procinterrupts.c: isblank()/isdigit() were passed a plain char. char is
   signed on x86-64, so any byte >= 0x80 becomes negative and the ctype
   lookup indexes out of bounds -- undefined behaviour. Four call sites cast
   to unsigned char.

============================ PERFORMANCE ============================

Measured with callgrind on a steady-state daemon (not a one-shot run: a
one-shot is dominated by process startup and hides all of this).

5. get_irq_info() was a g_list_find_custom() linear walk over the whole
   interrupt database, called once per line of /proc/interrupts on every
   scan -- O(n^2) per cycle. Added a GHashTable index kept in exact sync
   with both interrupts_db and banned_irqs, including the removal path
   (leaving a stale entry would hand out a pointer to freed memory) and
   free_irq_db().
   Isolated benchmark, identical workload:
       n=5    list 37,000 Ir   hash 63,000 Ir   (hash loses, tiny n)
       n=40   list 1,416,000   hash 504,000     (2.8x faster)
       n=128  list 13,542,400  hash 1,612,800   (8.4x)
       n=256  list 53,299,200  hash 3,225,600   (16.5x)
   The fallback linear search is retained, so behaviour is unchanged if the
   index cannot be allocated.

6. parse_proc_interrupts() called strdup()+free() for EVERY interrupt on
   EVERY scan, but only the hotplug path (a cache miss on get_irq_info)
   ever reads the copy. Moved into that branch. Note the copy must be taken
   before the ':' is overwritten with a NUL, or strdup() truncates the line
   -- my first attempt got this wrong and the test caught it.

7. bitmap.h hweight*(): replaced the SWAR popcount with
   __builtin_popcount*(). __bitmap_weight() is the hottest own-code function
   in a steady-state profile (1.04% of all instructions, worst I1 miss rate
   of any own-code function). llvm-mca -mcpu=raptorlake, 1000 iterations:
   12015 -> 2005 cycles for the 64-bit case. Confirmed 6 POPCNT instructions
   are emitted once the build passes an arch flag; the builtin degrades to an
   equivalent sequence on targets without POPCNT, so it is never worse.
   Measured in situ: __bitmap_weight -44% instructions per scan cycle.

8. get_irq_data()/get_object_stat(): the UI "stats" reply was assembled with
   strlen()+realloc() once per IRQ, which is quadratic in the interrupt
   count. Rebuilt on GString. (This part was already right in v1 and is
   carried over.)

============================ BEHAVIOUR ============================

9. Adaptive scan interval, implemented correctly this time. v1 shipped:
       int user_min = sleep_interval;
       if (sleep_interval > adaptive) sleep_interval = adaptive;
       if (sleep_interval < user_min) sleep_interval = user_min;
   user_min is captured FROM sleep_interval, so the second clamp always
   restored the original: verified inert across all 20 tested
   (start, max_diff) combinations. v1's 1024-entry cache was also dead code
   (its overflow branch was unreachable) and served stale load on alternating
   cycles.
   Now: one allocation-free pass, no cache, interval 60 -> 5 -> 2 -> 1 s as
   load rises, clamped to [-t floor, 60]. Observed effect on an idle machine:
   ~12x fewer wakeups than a fixed 1 s interval.
   -t and the UI "settings sleep" both raise the floor.
   IRQBALANCE_NO_ADAPTIVE=1 pins the interval for deterministic interaction
   with an external IRQ-pinning tool.

10. v1 changed the default sleep_interval from 10 to 60 unconditionally.
    Reverted; 60 is now the adaptive ceiling, which is where that intent
    belonged.

11. v1's "Raptor Lake L2 prefetch" in build_object_tree() emitted ZERO
    prefetch instructions (verified with objdump on the built object), and
    prefetched a GList head pointer rather than the tree, in a function that
    runs once at startup. Dropped.

============================ VALIDATION ============================

  patch applies to pristine upstream 16844fb : clean, 0 fuzz
  clang 19 build                             : 0 errors, 0 warnings
  valgrind memcheck                          : 0 errors, 0 definitely-lost
  ASan, 22 socket commands + 8 underflow     : 0 errors
  UBSan, same                                : 0 findings (1 before the fix)
  fls() equivalence                          : 1,376,188 inputs, 0 mismatches
  hash index vs linear search                : identical DB, deterministic
